Hello Intel wireless team,

I'm hitting a highly reproducible firmware crash on Intel Wi-Fi 6 AX101 
and would like to help get it fixed. Details below.

== Hardware / environment ==
- Device: Intel Raptor Lake PCH CNVi WiFi, PCI 8086:51f1 rev 0x370, 
subsystem 8086:0244, rfid 0x10c000 (RF HR1 B3)
   Detected as "Intel(R) Wi-Fi 6 AX101"
- Firmware: 89.123cf747.0 so-a0-hr-b0-89.ucode (op_mode iwlmvm)
- linux-firmware: 20260319.git217ca6e4.1ubuntu
- Kernel: 7.0.0-29-generic (Ubuntu 26.04)
- Module options already applied (do not prevent the crash): iwlwifi 
disable_11ax=1 uapsd_disable=1 power_save=0
- AP: 5 GHz, channel 36, 80 MHz, WPA2+WPA3 (mixed/PSK), single AP, no 
roaming

== Symptoms ==
- "Microcode SW error detected. Restarting 0x0" — always within ~1 
second after "associated"
   (or during the re-connect attempt after a previous crash), causing a 
deauth/reassoc loop
   of 2-6 crashes in a row until an association finally survives. Once 
connected, the link
   is stable for hours (100+ MB transferred without issues).
- ~212 crashes over one month of logs on this boot alone.

== Reproducibility ==
Loop of 10 `nmcli dev disconnect; nmcli dev wifi connect` cycles: 2/10 
associations crashed
(~20%). Log with timestamps attached (repro-test-10-reconnects.log).

== Crash signature (identical every time) ==
- 0x00000071 | NMI_INTERRUPT_UMAC_FATAL
- UMAC CURRENT PC: 0x804a237c (same every crash)
- LMAC1 CURRENT PC: 0xd0
- ADVANCED_SYSASSERT 0x2010120E, umac branchlink2 0x804824F0, last host 
cmd 0x00CD020F
- IML/ROM error/state 0x00000B03, FSEQ_ERROR_CODE 0x60000000
Full error log dump attached (crash-context-2026-09-14-1834.log).

== Additional finding ==
With iwlwifi-so-a0-hr-b0-89.ucode removed from /lib/firmware, the driver 
refuses to load
("iwlwifi-so-a0-hr-b0-89 is required", "no suitable firmware found") 
instead of falling
back to the older ucode files (86/83/...) present on the system — so 
users cannot even
work around this by pinning an older firmware on this kernel.

Happy to run additional tests, capture monitor logs, or try patched 
firmware.

Best regards, Mike

